  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\int_0^x \\sqrt{1-\\left(y^{\\prime}(t)\\right)^2} d t=\\int_0^x y(t) d t, 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, y \\geq 0, y(0)=0$. Then at $x=2, y^{\\prime \\prime}+y+1$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hfOEZ",
      "text": "1",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "st1cN",
      "text": "2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "gYnSP",
      "text": "$\\sqrt{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "7M3Dv",
      "text": "$1 / 2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\sqrt{1-\\left(y^{\\prime}(x)\\right)^2}=y(x) \\\\ & 1-\\left(\\frac{d y}{d x}\\right)^2=y^2 \\\\ & \\left(\\frac{d y}{d x}\\right)^2=1-y^2\\end{aligned}$\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{d y}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}=d x \\text { OR } \\frac{d y}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}=-d x \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\sin ^{-1} y=x+c, \\sin ^{-1} y=-x+c \\\\ & x=0, y=0 \\Rightarrow c=0 \\\\ & \\sin ^{-1} y=x, \\text { as } y \\geq 0 \\\\ & \\sin x=y \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\frac{d y}{d x}=\\cos x \\\\ & \\frac{d^2 y}{d x^2}=-\\sin x \\\\ & \\Rightarrow-\\sin x+\\sin x+1=1\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
